WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:18.385
Let's get started.

00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:22.522
We're going to keep talking
about polymers today.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:26.693
Polymers-- that's also going
to be the topic of the quiz.

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:32.198
And because of the exam
and everything-- and so

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.934
we're having our second
lecture on polymers today.

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.804
The quiz tomorrow will be
at the end of recitation

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:40.640
so that you can have a
full second recitation

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.909
on this topic.

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:43.410
OK.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:47.814
I mentioned this
on Monday, right?

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:50.483
Monday we introduced
polymers and I

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:54.254
covered two different
ways to make polymers, OK?

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:58.825
Let's write those down.

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:03.430
Let's write those down with
chalk, which is somewhere.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.299
Here we go.

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:15.909
On the one hand, we had what
we called radical initiation.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:21.146
So we use a radical initiator.

00:01:21.146 --> 00:01:22.649
Remember this R dot?

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:25.652
And we talked
about radicals, OK?

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:29.722
And how that can lead to
this kind of chain reaction.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:30.223
Right?

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:35.395
And so this is called
chain polymerization.

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:39.065
Sometimes it's called
addition, polymerization.

00:01:39.065 --> 00:01:43.570
But it all involves
a radical to start it

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.338
and then a double bond.

00:01:45.338 --> 00:01:47.140
Remember, we talked
about that double bond.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.043
The importance of
the double bond.

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:50.543
Right.

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:53.146
So this would have some monomer.

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.448
This would have some monomer.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:03.156
But it's got to have
with a double bond.

00:02:03.156 --> 00:02:05.158
That's not an equal sign,
that's a double bond.

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.926
Now, the second
way we talked about

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:09.529
was called condensation
polymerization.

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:14.267
So condensation
polymerization these

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:21.407
are two ways we covered that
allow you to make these really,

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:23.209
really long chains.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:26.312
That's what a polymer is, right?

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:29.482
Polymer-- "mer" being
from say hundreds

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:33.386
to thousands and even millions.

00:02:33.386 --> 00:02:35.855
And in condensation
polymerization,

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:40.793
you would have two different--
you could even have more.

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:41.494
--monomers.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:47.767
And these two different
monomers, well,

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:48.835
let's write it explicitly.

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:51.337
They react, OK?

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:55.341
And they react because
they've got these end groups

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:58.311
that when they see
each other, right,

00:02:58.311 --> 00:03:03.283
from one monomer to the
next, they form a bond.

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:05.919
And in the case that
we did on Monday,

00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:08.721
they formed an amide bond.

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:10.056
And they gave off water, right?

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:12.125
So water left.

00:03:12.125 --> 00:03:14.594
So when you do condensation
polymerization,

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:16.396
you start with two
different monomers.

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:18.064
They've got the
end groups on them

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:20.133
that when they see each
other, they want to react.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:25.004
You wind up with a polymer
that weighs a little bit less

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.639
than the two monomers, right?

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:29.509
Because you've given off water.

00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:31.010
You could give off other things.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.111
There's a lot of examples.

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:38.117
So in the reaction you give
off maybe H2O, like we did.

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:42.055
Could be HCL.

00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:43.022
Could be NH3.

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:44.224
There are a number of ways.

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:45.692
I don't want you
to get the feeling

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:48.561
that the only way you can do
condensation polymerization

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:51.631
is the nylon example, all right?

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:54.634
There's a lot of
ways you can do this.

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:56.668
But one thing that
is certainly true

00:03:56.668 --> 00:04:01.674
is that in forming that bond,
you give something else up.

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:05.445
And that's why sometimes people
like calling this addition.

00:04:05.445 --> 00:04:07.480
Because in addition
polymerization,

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.619
right, you take a monomer
and you just keep adding it.

00:04:12.619 --> 00:04:16.723
And the sum is n times
the monomer, right?

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:18.124
The sum of the weight.

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:20.326
Whereas in this case,
it's a little bit less

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:22.862
because you did a reaction.

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:23.763
OK.

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.832
So that's all kind of
getting us back in the mood.

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:28.501
That's what we did on Monday.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:32.905
Now today, I want to talk about
the properties of polymers

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:33.940
and how we control them.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:34.907
How we can change them.

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.109
Different ways that
we can change them.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:37.644
I can't cover
everything but I want

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:40.880
to cover some key elements
of engineering polymers.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.682
So we're going to talk
about the properties

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:46.753
and a couple of specific things
that we use to control them.

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:51.457
So let's start a list
here in the middle.

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:55.128
And the first thing is
something we already

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:58.431
did talk about Monday, which is
just the monomer itself, right?

00:04:58.431 --> 00:05:00.933
So what is it that
matters in a polymer?

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:06.539
What is it that dictates what
a polymer will be like, right?

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:09.942
Well, one is the monomer.

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:11.711
So that's the unit--

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:12.378
well, OK.

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:14.047
We just said that
you need two here.

00:05:14.047 --> 00:05:16.983
So maybe there's monomers.

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:18.751
But that's the unit
that you're repeating.

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:20.953
Clearly that's going to be
really important, right?

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:23.089
What did you put there, right?

00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:25.224
Is it C2H4?

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:27.026
Is it polyethylene or
is it something else?

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:28.194
Did you add something to it?

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:30.863
The benzene ring, right?

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:33.099
And so that's going to
be really important.

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:34.667
The other thing that
we talked about--

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.436
and so we're going to
create this list --is

00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:37.503
the molecular weight.

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:44.043
Now remember, molecular weight.

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:46.946
Oh we, like every
little community,

00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:48.781
likes to come up
with its own names.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:50.650
We know grams per mole.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:54.120
And then they say,
oh that's a dalton.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.989
And so now we have to say, OK,
a dalton is a gram per mole.

00:05:56.989 --> 00:06:00.693
Kilodalton would be
1,000 grams per mole.

00:06:00.693 --> 00:06:03.396
But we also said
that this gives us

00:06:03.396 --> 00:06:06.165
the degree of polymerization.

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:07.567
So that's really
important, right?

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:12.872
Because how much to the
degree of polarization--

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:17.810
I mean, if I know
what my mer is and I

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:22.248
know the molecular weight, the
grams per mole of a strand,

00:06:22.248 --> 00:06:24.450
then I know how many
mers went into it.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.152
So I know the degree
of polymerization.

00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:27.887
That's just n, right?

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:29.355
It's some number.

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:35.094
But this is important
and I'll put it over here

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:37.196
so I can keep going
down on that board.

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:41.933
When you have a
synthesis of a polymer,

00:06:41.933 --> 00:06:44.070
doesn't matter which
one of those you use,

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.606
you're going out 100,000 units.

00:06:46.606 --> 00:06:48.908
Yeah, I can't say
well, every single one

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:49.976
is going to be 100,000.

00:06:49.976 --> 00:06:51.544
That is really,
really hard to do.

00:06:51.544 --> 00:06:53.679
So instead you get
a distribution.

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:55.615
So when you say the
molecular weight, what you

00:06:55.615 --> 00:06:58.683
mean is some average, right?

00:06:58.683 --> 00:07:01.821
So this would be like maybe
the molecular weight, maybe

00:07:01.821 --> 00:07:05.158
this is in daltons, maybe
it's in kilodaltons.

00:07:05.158 --> 00:07:09.395
And you say, aw, the molecular
weight is 40 kilodaltons.

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:11.964
No, that's the average.

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:14.934
You might have ones that are
way, way longer out here.

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:17.336
Maybe orders of
magnitude longer.

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:22.141
This distribution can
be orders of magnitude.

00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:29.615
But it still can
be you know, you

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:32.118
can try to make them longer so
you have a distribution that's

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:32.784
more out here.

00:07:32.784 --> 00:07:34.120
Or you make them shorter, right?

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.289
So this is something
that is important

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:37.723
and that can be engineered.

00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:40.760
You can imagine if my chains
are really, really long,

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.563
then maybe it's a
tougher material, right?

00:07:43.563 --> 00:07:47.233
Because there's just more
covalently bonded backbone

00:07:47.233 --> 00:07:49.001
to break, right?

00:07:49.001 --> 00:07:52.305
You can imagine that
that might be one thing.

00:07:52.305 --> 00:07:55.341
Oh, but the thing that
we gotta talk about

00:07:55.341 --> 00:07:59.011
is the chemistry which
dictates the bonding.

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:03.749
OK, so the chemistry.

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:05.651
So the interactions.

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:10.523
And this takes us back
to so many happy moments

00:08:10.523 --> 00:08:14.560
from this fall where we talked
about intermolecular forces.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.463
All of those apply to polymers.

00:08:17.463 --> 00:08:19.065
They all apply.

00:08:19.065 --> 00:08:20.032
What are the IMFs?

00:08:23.369 --> 00:08:26.906
Well, if I gave you an
example, like a question--

00:08:26.906 --> 00:08:30.209
OK, maybe you could
say, well, let's see--

00:08:30.209 --> 00:08:32.211
OK, I've got polyethylene.

00:08:38.584 --> 00:08:40.486
Remember, this how we write it?

00:08:40.486 --> 00:08:42.755
And in this case,
I've left off my Hs.

00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:44.457
But the little sticks
there means there's

00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:47.193
an H above and below each one.

00:08:47.193 --> 00:08:49.295
I've got polystyrene.

00:08:49.295 --> 00:08:50.663
So that's polyethylene.

00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:53.833
Here's polystyrene.

00:08:53.833 --> 00:08:57.270
Remember, this one
has a benzene ring.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:00.206
Oh, that's a terrible
drawing of a benzene ring.

00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:02.608
But it's what it's
supposed to be.

00:09:02.608 --> 00:09:05.945
They have to come out
of the parentheses.

00:09:05.945 --> 00:09:07.647
If it doesn't come
out of the parentheses

00:09:07.647 --> 00:09:11.551
is not a polymer because you're
not indicating repetition,

00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:12.418
right?

00:09:12.418 --> 00:09:15.087
OK, that's supposed
to be benzene.

00:09:15.087 --> 00:09:18.958
And that'll be polystyrene.

00:09:18.958 --> 00:09:21.627
And then how about another one?

00:09:21.627 --> 00:09:22.962
I'll put it here.

00:09:22.962 --> 00:09:25.898
How about polyvinyl alcohol?

00:09:25.898 --> 00:09:27.533
So now watch this.

00:09:27.533 --> 00:09:32.305
I'm going brackets
because remember, it's OK.

00:09:32.305 --> 00:09:36.876
And so here OH, right?

00:09:36.876 --> 00:09:42.014
And each one of these would
have some number of repeats.

00:09:42.014 --> 00:09:45.318
That's polyvinyl alcohol.

00:09:45.318 --> 00:09:49.288
I can say well, just
look at those monomers.

00:09:49.288 --> 00:09:51.924
Just look at the mers, right?

00:09:51.924 --> 00:09:54.594
Remember, the
monomers if these were

00:09:54.594 --> 00:09:57.563
made with chain polarization,
the monomers would

00:09:57.563 --> 00:09:58.831
have a double bond, right?

00:09:58.831 --> 00:10:00.132
Remember that.

00:10:00.132 --> 00:10:03.269
But so this is the repeat
unit in the polymer.

00:10:03.269 --> 00:10:06.739
That's what goes into the
brackets or the parentheses.

00:10:06.739 --> 00:10:09.442
I can look at it and
say, which one of these

00:10:09.442 --> 00:10:13.846
is going to be the
strongest mechanically?

00:10:13.846 --> 00:10:17.750
And you know from your IMF days.

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:20.853
You know which one it is
because only one of them

00:10:20.853 --> 00:10:23.623
can form a stronger
bond than London.

00:10:23.623 --> 00:10:25.157
They're all going
to have London.

00:10:25.157 --> 00:10:28.060
Everyone has London.

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:31.330
But this one has
hydrogen as well.

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:32.498
That's got hydrogen bonds.

00:10:32.498 --> 00:10:37.036
So the polyvinyl alcohol
will be stronger, right?

00:10:37.036 --> 00:10:38.304
That will be the stronger one.

00:10:38.304 --> 00:10:41.173
And you know that bond
is going to be in there.

00:10:41.173 --> 00:10:43.809
It's going to bond to other
hydrogens from other chains

00:10:43.809 --> 00:10:45.945
or maybe from itself.

00:10:45.945 --> 00:10:49.348
Because remember this
is what it looks like.

00:10:49.348 --> 00:10:52.018
But even much, much,
much, much, much more.

00:10:52.018 --> 00:10:54.920
These are macromolecules.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:56.622
These are macromolecules.

00:10:56.622 --> 00:10:58.357
OK.

00:10:58.357 --> 00:10:58.891
Yes.

00:10:58.891 --> 00:11:04.263
But now I wanted to put
these here so I had them.

00:11:04.263 --> 00:11:06.298
So I'm going to
take one of these.

00:11:06.298 --> 00:11:06.999
Which one is it?

00:11:06.999 --> 00:11:09.135
It's polystyrene.

00:11:09.135 --> 00:11:14.173
It's this one with the very
poorly drawn benzene ring. .

00:11:14.173 --> 00:11:16.709
And I didn't change
anything about it

00:11:16.709 --> 00:11:18.744
but look at what it can do.

00:11:18.744 --> 00:11:23.482
That's polystyrene in
three different forms.

00:11:23.482 --> 00:11:26.218
They're all cups but you
know these cups well, right?

00:11:26.218 --> 00:11:27.453
One's Styrofoam.

00:11:27.453 --> 00:11:29.288
One, I don't know
what you call that.

00:11:29.288 --> 00:11:32.491
But they're all
plastic cups but they

00:11:32.491 --> 00:11:34.694
have very different properties.

00:11:34.694 --> 00:11:37.997
They have the
exact same polymer.

00:11:37.997 --> 00:11:39.899
Polystyrene.

00:11:39.899 --> 00:11:41.133
How do we do that?

00:11:41.133 --> 00:11:42.368
That's not the IMFs.

00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:45.237
It doesn't even have
to have anything

00:11:45.237 --> 00:11:46.472
to do with a molecular weight.

00:11:46.472 --> 00:11:48.207
It could.

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:50.109
No, it's something else.

00:11:50.109 --> 00:11:54.080
And that is the density.

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:57.683
Oh and so now we get
to talk about density

00:11:57.683 --> 00:12:00.786
and crystallinity.

00:12:00.786 --> 00:12:05.825
And I just saved three letters.

00:12:05.825 --> 00:12:07.393
Makes me very happy.

00:12:07.393 --> 00:12:15.401
Now crystallinity
density, memories.

00:12:15.401 --> 00:12:16.902
Good memories.

00:12:16.902 --> 00:12:21.040
Very good memories are
happening right now

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:23.843
from when we talked
about amorphous materials

00:12:23.843 --> 00:12:27.780
and the glass transition, right?

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:31.517
Because remember, I
drew this on the board.

00:12:31.517 --> 00:12:32.218
Let's see.

00:12:32.218 --> 00:12:36.722
Let's get these to come down.

00:12:36.722 --> 00:12:38.790
What that actually looks like--

00:12:42.161 --> 00:12:45.231
each strand in there,
remember, goes like this.

00:12:45.231 --> 00:12:46.398
And then it's like, oh wait.

00:12:46.398 --> 00:12:48.601
I can make a crystal.

00:12:48.601 --> 00:12:49.702
And then it's like, nope.

00:12:49.702 --> 00:12:50.669
I'm amorphous again.

00:12:50.669 --> 00:12:51.804
And then it's like oh wait.

00:12:51.804 --> 00:12:52.838
Hold on.

00:12:52.838 --> 00:12:54.573
I can crystallize right here.

00:12:54.573 --> 00:12:57.576
And then it's amorphous
again and it's so fun.

00:12:57.576 --> 00:13:00.379
And then you go
like this and that.

00:13:00.379 --> 00:13:04.984
And that's like one one
hundredth of a strand.

00:13:04.984 --> 00:13:11.524
And so you know the
crystalline region--

00:13:11.524 --> 00:13:12.858
what do I mean by "crystalline?"

00:13:12.858 --> 00:13:15.661
Well, the strand is
stacking up in some

00:13:15.661 --> 00:13:17.997
ordered regular repeating way.

00:13:17.997 --> 00:13:19.231
What do I mean by "amorphous?"

00:13:19.231 --> 00:13:22.968
Well, I mean spaghetti.

00:13:22.968 --> 00:13:25.504
But a polymer, in
its own strand,

00:13:25.504 --> 00:13:30.376
can crystallize to some extent
or to more or less, right?

00:13:30.376 --> 00:13:31.877
And that's something
we can control.

00:13:31.877 --> 00:13:33.846
We can engineer that.

00:13:33.846 --> 00:13:36.582
And that's what leads
to these differences

00:13:36.582 --> 00:13:41.153
because you know that
in a crystalline region,

00:13:41.153 --> 00:13:46.192
it's going to have a
lower volume, right?

00:13:46.192 --> 00:13:48.594
It's going to take
up less volume.

00:13:48.594 --> 00:13:50.963
That's what we did
before with glasses.

00:13:50.963 --> 00:13:53.833
So let's put that
on the board, right?

00:13:53.833 --> 00:13:58.571
So you know that from
out good old days

00:13:58.571 --> 00:14:04.510
that if I melt a material,
well, it could form a crystal.

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:05.945
That's temperature.

00:14:05.945 --> 00:14:09.215
And this would be
volume per mole.

00:14:09.215 --> 00:14:11.016
Right, this would be
like the melting point.

00:14:11.016 --> 00:14:12.084
It might form a crystal.

00:14:12.084 --> 00:14:14.787
But with polymers, imagine
I've got the spaghetti strands

00:14:14.787 --> 00:14:16.822
and now they're miles long.

00:14:16.822 --> 00:14:19.491
It's really hard
to line it all up.

00:14:19.491 --> 00:14:24.496
And so a lot of it will
not find that place.

00:14:24.496 --> 00:14:27.833
And instead it will
be a glass, which

00:14:27.833 --> 00:14:32.104
you know is another way of
saying so these are now TGs,

00:14:32.104 --> 00:14:32.605
right?

00:14:32.605 --> 00:14:34.506
TG.

00:14:34.506 --> 00:14:37.977
And so you know now
all about this, right?

00:14:37.977 --> 00:14:40.512
And you know that
cooling rate is

00:14:40.512 --> 00:14:43.649
one way to change the density.

00:14:43.649 --> 00:14:48.454
To change the volume
per mole, right?

00:14:48.454 --> 00:14:52.024
So the density of
the amorphous region

00:14:52.024 --> 00:14:56.095
is one parameter that could
be glassy in different ways,

00:14:56.095 --> 00:14:56.996
like we did before.

00:14:56.996 --> 00:15:00.032
And then how much of it
you can make into a crystal

00:15:00.032 --> 00:15:01.934
is another parameter.

00:15:01.934 --> 00:15:04.937
Both of which play into
the overall density

00:15:04.937 --> 00:15:06.872
and crystallinity, right?

00:15:06.872 --> 00:15:09.575
Both of those are
extremely important

00:15:09.575 --> 00:15:13.012
and those are going to depend on
all sorts of parameters related

00:15:13.012 --> 00:15:16.015
to the processing and the
temperature and so forth.

00:15:16.015 --> 00:15:18.817
Those are the things that
can lead to changes like this

00:15:18.817 --> 00:15:21.553
from the same exact chemistry.

00:15:21.553 --> 00:15:29.028
So let's go from polystyrene
back to polyethylene.

00:15:29.028 --> 00:15:31.730
So if you look at polyethylene--

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:32.464
so this is a list.

00:15:32.464 --> 00:15:34.600
I showed I think a
version of this on Monday.

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:39.038
It's hard to read.

00:15:39.038 --> 00:15:39.972
Don't worry about it.

00:15:39.972 --> 00:15:42.841
These are all monomers.

00:15:42.841 --> 00:15:43.842
And this is the polymer.

00:15:43.842 --> 00:15:45.110
They didn't put
the brackets there

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:46.679
but you can see--
oh maybe they did.

00:15:46.679 --> 00:15:47.813
Yeah sort of.

00:15:47.813 --> 00:15:51.317
You can see the line,
right, coming out.

00:15:51.317 --> 00:15:52.952
That's the repeat line.

00:15:52.952 --> 00:15:54.520
And this is the monomer.

00:15:54.520 --> 00:16:00.159
Notice the double bonds ready
for chain polymerization.

00:16:00.159 --> 00:16:02.261
And here are the properties
and here are the uses.

00:16:02.261 --> 00:16:06.432
Look at this-- LDPE and HDPE.

00:16:06.432 --> 00:16:08.233
Now LDPE and HDPE.

00:16:08.233 --> 00:16:13.138
So that's low density
and high density

00:16:13.138 --> 00:16:15.040
of the same exact polymer.

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:18.744
So the interactions
are the same.

00:16:18.744 --> 00:16:20.779
But all we've done is
we've changed the density

00:16:20.779 --> 00:16:23.849
and the difference between
the properties is tremendous.

00:16:23.849 --> 00:16:25.417
So when you go to
the grocery store

00:16:25.417 --> 00:16:29.054
and they give you a plastic bag,
which you'll wind up feeding

00:16:29.054 --> 00:16:34.159
to fishes in the ocean as
you now know, it's very soft.

00:16:34.159 --> 00:16:36.095
It's the exact same
material that's

00:16:36.095 --> 00:16:40.799
in over 30% of all
toys on the planet.

00:16:40.799 --> 00:16:42.301
It's still polyethylene.

00:16:42.301 --> 00:16:45.771
All we've done is
mess with that.

00:16:45.771 --> 00:16:49.508
We've messed with the density.

00:16:49.508 --> 00:16:51.577
So how do you mess
with the density?

00:16:51.577 --> 00:16:52.511
How?

00:16:52.511 --> 00:16:55.647
Well, it turns out
with polymers there are

00:16:55.647 --> 00:16:57.349
different ways you can do this.

00:16:57.349 --> 00:17:00.152
One we just talked about.

00:17:00.152 --> 00:17:04.189
So one, let's do a little--

00:17:04.189 --> 00:17:05.424
I'm going to do this.

00:17:05.424 --> 00:17:11.030
So one would be
let's say processing.

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:12.131
I'll call it processing.

00:17:16.234 --> 00:17:17.269
That's right here.

00:17:17.269 --> 00:17:19.070
How quickly did I melt it?

00:17:19.070 --> 00:17:21.839
What were the conditions
that I formed it in?

00:17:21.839 --> 00:17:24.777
But see you can imagine
changing its ability

00:17:24.777 --> 00:17:27.613
to crystallize our pack
by just changing something

00:17:27.613 --> 00:17:32.885
else about the polymer itself.

00:17:32.885 --> 00:17:35.587
And so another way
to do that would

00:17:35.587 --> 00:17:40.325
be to change the physical
structure of the chain.

00:17:40.325 --> 00:17:41.794
Physical structure.

00:17:45.731 --> 00:17:49.101
And there are two ways
that you can do this.

00:17:49.101 --> 00:17:50.035
Oh boy, let's see.

00:17:50.035 --> 00:17:52.838
How am I going to continue this?

00:17:52.838 --> 00:17:56.108
OK let's do this.

00:17:56.108 --> 00:17:57.376
Bullet one.

00:17:57.376 --> 00:17:59.478
One way is called branching.

00:18:03.282 --> 00:18:05.684
And another is called tacticity.

00:18:08.687 --> 00:18:09.822
So what are these?

00:18:09.822 --> 00:18:14.893
So branching is exactly what it
sounds like because the polymer

00:18:14.893 --> 00:18:15.828
I showed you here--

00:18:18.464 --> 00:18:20.966
this is a mess and it's showing
how it's a spaghetti chain.

00:18:20.966 --> 00:18:24.069
But notice that it's
just a line everywhere.

00:18:24.069 --> 00:18:28.440
We would call this
a straight line.

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:31.877
This is a straight line, it
just kind of wanders around.

00:18:31.877 --> 00:18:32.644
Yeah.

00:18:32.644 --> 00:18:33.679
OK.

00:18:33.679 --> 00:18:39.952
Because it's in contrast to--

00:18:39.952 --> 00:18:43.522
if it's a straight line, then
it's sort of locally straight.

00:18:43.522 --> 00:18:44.690
But what if it did this?

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:49.695
And then it's like,
well, which way do I go?

00:18:53.332 --> 00:18:55.134
You can be the
exact same chemistry

00:18:55.134 --> 00:18:58.470
but because maybe one of those
radicals in the soup while this

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:01.673
was being made came in and
got to move a hydrogen around

00:19:01.673 --> 00:19:02.774
in just the right way.

00:19:02.774 --> 00:19:05.511
It was able to start growing
off of the side, right?

00:19:05.511 --> 00:19:08.213
That's a branch.

00:19:08.213 --> 00:19:13.218
So you can imagine that
controlling this branching

00:19:13.218 --> 00:19:15.521
would be a really
big deal in terms

00:19:15.521 --> 00:19:18.891
of the crystallinity right?

00:19:18.891 --> 00:19:23.295
So now if you go to pasta.

00:19:23.295 --> 00:19:25.898
It always seems to
come back to pasta.

00:19:25.898 --> 00:19:27.699
Maybe it's just me.

00:19:27.699 --> 00:19:28.700
I don't think so though.

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:33.438
I think there's something
deep about the pasta in here.

00:19:33.438 --> 00:19:37.543
But see, this is a polymer.

00:19:37.543 --> 00:19:42.080
But you can imagine
now if I make spaghetti

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:45.551
and it looks like the
left, it's all packed in.

00:19:45.551 --> 00:19:48.654
This is actually an interesting
idea for a new pasta brand,

00:19:48.654 --> 00:19:49.388
right?

00:19:49.388 --> 00:19:53.792
And what if each spaghetti
strand looked like that?

00:19:53.792 --> 00:19:58.230
And now you take those
and you try to make your--

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:01.767
it would be actually pretty cool
but it wouldn't pack as well.

00:20:01.767 --> 00:20:04.636
You can feel it with
the pasta, right?

00:20:04.636 --> 00:20:05.304
You can feel it.

00:20:05.304 --> 00:20:07.973
It's going to take up
more room in the bowl.

00:20:07.973 --> 00:20:11.243
I mean, it's the exact
same thing with polymers.

00:20:11.243 --> 00:20:15.080
So if you want a
polymer to be stronger,

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:18.817
to have maybe more
crystallinity or higher density,

00:20:18.817 --> 00:20:24.022
HDPE, then you don't
want very much branching.

00:20:24.022 --> 00:20:25.490
So you better dial that in.

00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:26.892
When you synthesize
it, you better

00:20:26.892 --> 00:20:28.827
think carefully about
how you're making

00:20:28.827 --> 00:20:34.099
this to prevent those kinds of
side chains from growing, OK?

00:20:34.099 --> 00:20:36.101
Well, tacticity
is another thing.

00:20:36.101 --> 00:20:38.737
And that's not the
same as branching.

00:20:38.737 --> 00:20:43.742
Tacticity is remember-- ah!

00:20:43.742 --> 00:20:45.477
Where did it go?

00:20:45.477 --> 00:20:46.245
I had it up there.

00:20:49.348 --> 00:20:52.084
There's the benzene ring.

00:20:52.084 --> 00:20:56.588
Are they all on the same side or
are they on alternating sides?

00:20:56.588 --> 00:20:58.724
How did this thing go?

00:20:58.724 --> 00:21:02.928
Did two go on the same side
and then one and is it random?

00:21:02.928 --> 00:21:06.965
I mean, you can imagine
that if I have a way

00:21:06.965 --> 00:21:10.202
to break the symmetry
of the chain like this,

00:21:10.202 --> 00:21:15.641
then you can imagine that if I
break it a lot so I'm atactic,

00:21:15.641 --> 00:21:19.544
then that's going to be
harder to line up as well.

00:21:19.544 --> 00:21:22.247
That's going to make it
harder for the spaghetti

00:21:22.247 --> 00:21:26.051
to kind of fall right
next to each other.

00:21:26.051 --> 00:21:28.520
But even if this
spaghetti had little

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:31.390
you can call these little,
mini benzine branches.

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:33.125
It's not branching.

00:21:33.125 --> 00:21:34.693
But you can call
them little branches.

00:21:34.693 --> 00:21:36.194
But if they're all
on the same side,

00:21:36.194 --> 00:21:40.732
then you can imagine they
can still stack really well.

00:21:40.732 --> 00:21:43.302
So when you have side
groups like this,

00:21:43.302 --> 00:21:46.505
this is another aspect of
engineering the polymer

00:21:46.505 --> 00:21:48.640
that you got to think
very carefully about.

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:52.277
Because if it's isotactic
or syndiotactic,

00:21:52.277 --> 00:21:53.945
then it's going to be--

00:21:53.945 --> 00:21:56.014
so that's the physical
structure of the chain.

00:21:56.014 --> 00:21:58.884
--then it's going to be
able to pack more easily

00:21:58.884 --> 00:22:01.953
and it's going to
be stronger, right?

00:22:01.953 --> 00:22:04.790
if it's atactic and we're
talking about like a hundred

00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:06.258
degrees.

00:22:06.258 --> 00:22:11.963
Same polymer, same chemical
unit make the molecular weights

00:22:11.963 --> 00:22:12.464
the same.

00:22:12.464 --> 00:22:16.635
All you've changed is whether
there's symmetry or not.

00:22:16.635 --> 00:22:19.871
And you can change the melting
point by a hundred degrees.

00:22:19.871 --> 00:22:21.039
Right, of the same material.

00:22:21.039 --> 00:22:23.642
So that's another
way that we engineer

00:22:23.642 --> 00:22:27.512
the properties of polymers, OK?

00:22:27.512 --> 00:22:32.084
Same polymer garbage
bags to Tupperware.

00:22:32.084 --> 00:22:33.585
Same chemistry.

00:22:33.585 --> 00:22:37.122
Isotactic to atactic.

00:22:37.122 --> 00:22:42.394
OK, now we get to
something really fun,

00:22:42.394 --> 00:22:44.329
which is cross-links.

00:22:44.329 --> 00:22:49.368
And this is number five, OK?

00:22:49.368 --> 00:22:54.072
So we're talking about
ways to control polymers.

00:22:54.072 --> 00:22:57.642
Cross-linking is one of the
single most important ways

00:22:57.642 --> 00:23:00.746
to engineer the
properties of polymers.

00:23:00.746 --> 00:23:02.714
And it basically
is exactly what it

00:23:02.714 --> 00:23:06.618
says-- you're linking
two different polymer

00:23:06.618 --> 00:23:08.887
chains together across.

00:23:08.887 --> 00:23:12.724
Cross linking, OK?

00:23:12.724 --> 00:23:14.826
Now, it turns out
the one-- remember,

00:23:14.826 --> 00:23:17.829
I showed you the
video of the nylon.

00:23:17.829 --> 00:23:19.164
It's called the nylon rope pole.

00:23:19.164 --> 00:23:20.866
If you want to see
that again, Google it.

00:23:20.866 --> 00:23:21.933
It's so cool.

00:23:21.933 --> 00:23:24.603
You have two liquids
with the two--

00:23:24.603 --> 00:23:27.472
it's a condensation
polymerization example.

00:23:27.472 --> 00:23:30.409
Two liquids with two
different monomers and you

00:23:30.409 --> 00:23:32.077
pull a solid out
of the interface.

00:23:32.077 --> 00:23:34.279
And that's this.

00:23:34.279 --> 00:23:35.747
That's exactly this.

00:23:35.747 --> 00:23:37.449
And those are the
molecules, these

00:23:37.449 --> 00:23:39.351
are the monomers that
were in the liquid.

00:23:39.351 --> 00:23:42.053
And there is nylon 6.6.

00:23:42.053 --> 00:23:43.989
Remember, it's because
you have a chain there.

00:23:43.989 --> 00:23:44.723
And this is what you get.

00:23:44.723 --> 00:23:45.924
And it keeps repeating.

00:23:45.924 --> 00:23:48.627
And there's your polymer.

00:23:48.627 --> 00:23:52.464
The reason I'm saying that
nylon actually cross-links

00:23:52.464 --> 00:23:54.132
is because it's
something very important.

00:23:54.132 --> 00:23:58.837
It's not just that these
have different interactions

00:23:58.837 --> 00:23:59.738
with each other.

00:23:59.738 --> 00:24:00.372
No.

00:24:00.372 --> 00:24:02.174
It's something a little
bit more than that.

00:24:02.174 --> 00:24:05.644
So if I look at this and
say, OK, here's a carbon

00:24:05.644 --> 00:24:07.212
and a nitrogen. I'm
going to just try

00:24:07.212 --> 00:24:09.915
to draw a part of this.

00:24:09.915 --> 00:24:13.251
Carbon, nitrogen,
there's a hydrogen,

00:24:13.251 --> 00:24:16.388
and oh boy, here we go.

00:24:16.388 --> 00:24:18.490
Here's an oxygen. OK.

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:19.257
And it goes on.

00:24:19.257 --> 00:24:20.992
Oh, I'm not going to
draw what's in there.

00:24:20.992 --> 00:24:22.127
It's 6.6.

00:24:22.127 --> 00:24:24.496
And then comes another.

00:24:24.496 --> 00:24:27.999
OK, nitrogen,
hydrogen, and then it

00:24:27.999 --> 00:24:33.305
goes up to a carbon,
oxygen, and then ah, 6.6.

00:24:33.305 --> 00:24:34.973
Or whatever it is, right?

00:24:34.973 --> 00:24:39.144
And then it goes to
a carbon and oxygen.

00:24:39.144 --> 00:24:40.545
This should have been like that.

00:24:40.545 --> 00:24:48.753
And this goes to nitrogen
and so on and so on.

00:24:48.753 --> 00:24:51.823
Oh remember, this is
called the amide bond.

00:24:51.823 --> 00:24:52.757
That's cool, right?

00:24:52.757 --> 00:24:53.925
That's the bond that formed.

00:24:57.295 --> 00:24:58.196
That's the amide bond.

00:24:58.196 --> 00:25:01.399
That's the bond that formed
this polyamide, right?

00:25:01.399 --> 00:25:03.668
We did this on Monday.

00:25:03.668 --> 00:25:06.571
But look at what happened.

00:25:06.571 --> 00:25:09.541
So if I have
another one of these

00:25:09.541 --> 00:25:11.209
that comes up beneath
it-- and I'm not

00:25:11.209 --> 00:25:12.444
going to draw the whole thing.

00:25:12.444 --> 00:25:16.214
I'm just going to draw that
part that has the amide bond.

00:25:16.214 --> 00:25:21.219
You can see that if I have an
oxygen here with the carbon

00:25:21.219 --> 00:25:27.092
and let's do oh boy,
nitrogen, and like that OK?

00:25:27.092 --> 00:25:31.129
And so on, then
if this lines up--

00:25:31.129 --> 00:25:33.932
but it can line up.

00:25:33.932 --> 00:25:37.369
And then over here
you've got a hydrogen.

00:25:37.369 --> 00:25:39.738
And so you can get this
kind of really nice line

00:25:39.738 --> 00:25:46.978
up to form a hydrogen
bond between these chains.

00:25:46.978 --> 00:25:47.846
You can.

00:25:47.846 --> 00:25:49.381
It can happen.

00:25:49.381 --> 00:25:52.384
And that can add, as we
know, that can add strength

00:25:52.384 --> 00:25:53.952
to the material.

00:25:53.952 --> 00:25:57.689
So that's a cross link because
it's a very specific linkage

00:25:57.689 --> 00:26:02.360
between these strands, right?

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:04.596
It's not just sort of an
average over different types

00:26:04.596 --> 00:26:06.164
of bonding.

00:26:06.164 --> 00:26:10.368
It's the specific link
that's in the chain.

00:26:10.368 --> 00:26:14.806
Well, it turns out that
that just seemed to happen.

00:26:14.806 --> 00:26:17.809
Because of the chemistry
that was there it can happen.

00:26:17.809 --> 00:26:21.246
But look, we can
also make it happen.

00:26:21.246 --> 00:26:25.250
We can engineer
polymers and we do

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:30.055
to have all sorts of
cross-linking chemistry.

00:26:30.055 --> 00:26:33.191
And it's really one of the
most single important ways

00:26:33.191 --> 00:26:35.727
to control the properties
of polymers, right?

00:26:35.727 --> 00:26:38.129
So I want to talk about
that a little bit.

00:26:38.129 --> 00:26:40.599
And I'm going to
do it with maybe

00:26:40.599 --> 00:26:47.339
the most well-used, at
least, example of what

00:26:47.339 --> 00:26:48.607
a cross-linker does.

00:26:48.607 --> 00:26:50.909
We're going to
talk about rubber.

00:26:50.909 --> 00:26:56.114
Now, rubber is a
natural polymer.

00:26:56.114 --> 00:26:58.516
You can get it out
of trees, right?

00:26:58.516 --> 00:27:00.452
I think maybe I have a picture.

00:27:00.452 --> 00:27:01.586
Yeah, I do.

00:27:01.586 --> 00:27:03.388
Oh, poor Goodyear.

00:27:03.388 --> 00:27:07.292
But we'll talk about
him in a minute.

00:27:07.292 --> 00:27:09.694
There's rubber coming
right out of a tree.

00:27:09.694 --> 00:27:13.865
And when we see rubber in this
class we think of isoprene.

00:27:13.865 --> 00:27:18.670
And isoprene is
this molecule, OK?

00:27:18.670 --> 00:27:20.205
So here's isoprene.

00:27:20.205 --> 00:27:26.244
There's a CH3 and then
there's a double bond

00:27:26.244 --> 00:27:30.548
to a CH2 and another
double bond to another CH2.

00:27:33.785 --> 00:27:44.396
Now, the polymer that is coming
from this rubber tree is what?

00:27:44.396 --> 00:27:46.097
It's polyisoprene, OK?

00:27:46.097 --> 00:27:50.502
So I've got two double bonds.

00:27:50.502 --> 00:27:52.303
We're going to talk
about that in a second.

00:27:52.303 --> 00:27:55.740
But I only need one
to polymerize this.

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:58.410
I only need one, right?

00:27:58.410 --> 00:28:04.215
And so if I think about this,
I can make a polymer out of it

00:28:04.215 --> 00:28:06.351
and the polymer
would look like this.

00:28:06.351 --> 00:28:08.319
This would be polyisoprene, OK?

00:28:08.319 --> 00:28:11.690
So H, I'll draw the Hs
in just so we keep track.

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:15.126
Here's another C.
And this has my--

00:28:15.126 --> 00:28:15.694
oh boy.

00:28:15.694 --> 00:28:18.963
I said I'd draw them
and now I have to.

00:28:18.963 --> 00:28:22.467
Once you commit, all
right, there it is.

00:28:22.467 --> 00:28:23.435
H, right?

00:28:23.435 --> 00:28:26.237
And then you've got the
double bond in there.

00:28:26.237 --> 00:28:28.173
And an H there.

00:28:28.173 --> 00:28:33.344
And then you've got
this CH2 unit there.

00:28:33.344 --> 00:28:37.582
Oh, and that is polyisoprene.

00:28:37.582 --> 00:28:40.418
It's not-- it looks complicated.

00:28:40.418 --> 00:28:44.155
It's got a CH3 unit
and some CH2 units.

00:28:44.155 --> 00:28:46.224
But here's the key--

00:28:46.224 --> 00:28:49.094
it's got left over,
a double bond.

00:28:49.094 --> 00:28:51.529
All right, that's the key.

00:28:51.529 --> 00:28:58.670
Now, you see, that double bond,
I can do more chemistry with.

00:28:58.670 --> 00:29:01.406
I can do cross-link
chemistry with.

00:29:05.376 --> 00:29:08.980
So I started with two
and I'm left with one

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:11.683
because I needed one
to make the 100,000

00:29:11.683 --> 00:29:14.619
long chains of these things,
right so this is isoprene.

00:29:17.222 --> 00:29:19.424
And this is polyisoprene.

00:29:19.424 --> 00:29:21.693
Yeah, but the thing is
that polyisoprene is not

00:29:21.693 --> 00:29:22.227
very useful.

00:29:24.896 --> 00:29:26.197
The Mayans used it.

00:29:26.197 --> 00:29:29.868
People tried to use
polyisoprene directly

00:29:29.868 --> 00:29:32.737
from nature for centuries.

00:29:32.737 --> 00:29:36.407
The Mayans would because
it's soft, you know?

00:29:36.407 --> 00:29:37.976
It's like a mold.

00:29:37.976 --> 00:29:40.044
It's this really cool mold.

00:29:40.044 --> 00:29:42.814
And probably, at some point,
somebody stepped in it.

00:29:42.814 --> 00:29:45.817
And they're like,
look, my footprint.

00:29:45.817 --> 00:29:46.885
And it stayed.

00:29:46.885 --> 00:29:50.054
And so then, what they did
is they made boots out of it.

00:29:50.054 --> 00:29:51.623
And so they would
put their whole foot

00:29:51.623 --> 00:29:54.759
inside of bucket of isoprene.

00:29:54.759 --> 00:29:58.062
and then it would sort of
mold really well, right?

00:29:58.062 --> 00:29:59.631
And they'd make shoes that way.

00:29:59.631 --> 00:30:03.001
The problem is people tried
to do this much later too.

00:30:03.001 --> 00:30:06.871
The problem with making a
boot out of natural rubber

00:30:06.871 --> 00:30:09.440
is that the natural rubber
doesn't hold together

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:10.241
very well.

00:30:10.241 --> 00:30:13.378
So like seriously, on
a hot day, the boot

00:30:13.378 --> 00:30:17.315
melts, which is
not what you want.

00:30:17.315 --> 00:30:19.784
People made clothing
out of it too.

00:30:19.784 --> 00:30:24.489
Jackets were made with
polyisoprene inside

00:30:24.489 --> 00:30:25.456
of two fibers.

00:30:25.456 --> 00:30:28.993
But then it would melt and get
like sticky, like, on a summer

00:30:28.993 --> 00:30:32.497
day or if it rained out.

00:30:32.497 --> 00:30:34.632
So you had some
issues with rubber.

00:30:34.632 --> 00:30:37.502
But people really wanted to make
something out of this stuff.

00:30:37.502 --> 00:30:39.103
It seemed really neat.

00:30:39.103 --> 00:30:44.242
It could kind of be strong
but then very moldable.

00:30:44.242 --> 00:30:47.378
And so along comes
Charles Goodyear.

00:30:47.378 --> 00:30:54.953
And he accidentally left rubber
on his stove with sulfur.

00:30:54.953 --> 00:30:56.921
And literally, the
way the story goes

00:30:56.921 --> 00:31:01.593
is his house started smelling
like burnt rubber, which

00:31:01.593 --> 00:31:06.798
was probably the first time
ever that smell was made.

00:31:06.798 --> 00:31:11.569
And what he found was that this
rubber was completely different

00:31:11.569 --> 00:31:14.239
when it was left on
the stove with sulfur.

00:31:14.239 --> 00:31:17.342
It all comes back
to the double bond.

00:31:17.342 --> 00:31:19.143
It all comes back
to the double bond.

00:31:19.143 --> 00:31:19.811
Why?

00:31:19.811 --> 00:31:22.747
Because now I've
got a place where

00:31:22.747 --> 00:31:25.149
I can add a covalent link.

00:31:25.149 --> 00:31:28.586
And that's exactly what
sulfur does, all right?

00:31:28.586 --> 00:31:34.659
So if I take this--

00:31:34.659 --> 00:31:35.727
I'll try to draw it again.

00:31:35.727 --> 00:31:39.297
I'm not going to draw
the Hs this time.

00:31:39.297 --> 00:31:43.801
So I've got this and here's
that special double bond, right?

00:31:43.801 --> 00:31:48.506
And then I've got this and this.

00:31:48.506 --> 00:31:50.575
OK, that's natural rubber.

00:31:50.575 --> 00:31:53.177
But now see, with
a double bond, I

00:31:53.177 --> 00:31:54.812
can-- remember, what are these?

00:31:54.812 --> 00:31:57.982
Well, these are four electrons?

00:31:57.982 --> 00:32:00.919
But if sulfur comes
along, I might

00:32:00.919 --> 00:32:07.292
be able to take two of them
and move them like this.

00:32:07.292 --> 00:32:09.627
If sulfur comes and
offers something

00:32:09.627 --> 00:32:12.263
that the carbon wants.

00:32:12.263 --> 00:32:13.965
Hey, come and bond with me.

00:32:13.965 --> 00:32:15.733
It'll be more fun.

00:32:15.733 --> 00:32:18.803
Maybe we'll go to a lower
energy state, right?

00:32:18.803 --> 00:32:20.571
And then the carbon's
like, you know what?

00:32:20.571 --> 00:32:21.940
I get that.

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:23.341
That sounds pretty cool.

00:32:23.341 --> 00:32:25.510
I'm going to give an electron
from this double bond.

00:32:25.510 --> 00:32:28.746
I get to stay connected here
with a single bond, right?

00:32:28.746 --> 00:32:32.283
And then I can be here
and here and the sulfur

00:32:32.283 --> 00:32:36.087
can provide that other electron
and form a nice single bond,

00:32:36.087 --> 00:32:36.587
right?

00:32:40.091 --> 00:32:41.693
Because there's
that double bond,

00:32:41.693 --> 00:32:46.431
you can do the chemistry to
create covalent strong bonds

00:32:46.431 --> 00:32:46.965
to sulfur.

00:32:46.965 --> 00:32:50.068
And then you get like, another
sulfur and another sulfur

00:32:50.068 --> 00:32:56.341
and so on and these can be
maybe 2, 3, 4 sulfur units long,

00:32:56.341 --> 00:32:59.010
depending on how you cook it.

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:00.211
And then this will come.

00:33:00.211 --> 00:33:03.047
And this comes down
and finds another one,

00:33:03.047 --> 00:33:05.083
finds another strand.

00:33:05.083 --> 00:33:07.018
And it finds the
double bond that's

00:33:07.018 --> 00:33:10.188
in another polyisoprene strand.

00:33:10.188 --> 00:33:15.526
That's called cross--
that's well, accidental.

00:33:15.526 --> 00:33:16.427
He was a tinkerer.

00:33:16.427 --> 00:33:17.862
And he played with
lots of things.

00:33:17.862 --> 00:33:20.965
And his greatest discovery
was completely by accident.

00:33:20.965 --> 00:33:22.533
And he never made
any money off of it.

00:33:22.533 --> 00:33:24.569
Goodyear Tire, this
is Charles Goodyear.

00:33:24.569 --> 00:33:26.537
Goodyear tires,
this is the company.

00:33:26.537 --> 00:33:31.075
It was called the Goodyear
Vulcanite Company.

00:33:31.075 --> 00:33:33.478
Goodyear called
it vulcanization.

00:33:33.478 --> 00:33:38.649
And the reason is Vulcan
is the Roman god for fire.

00:33:38.649 --> 00:33:42.020
And he accidentally
left it on the fire.

00:33:42.020 --> 00:33:46.190
And so that's called
vulcanization because of that.

00:33:46.190 --> 00:33:48.793
So this used to be the
Goodyear Vulcanite Company.

00:33:48.793 --> 00:33:51.963
It's now Goodyear Tires, right?

00:33:51.963 --> 00:33:52.697
And what happened?

00:33:52.697 --> 00:33:54.832
What happened is
this low sulfur bonds

00:33:54.832 --> 00:33:58.102
that came about accidentally,
the covalent bonds,

00:33:58.102 --> 00:34:00.338
held that material together.

00:34:00.338 --> 00:34:06.844
And it gave it a controllable
flexibility and hardness right?

00:34:06.844 --> 00:34:09.746
That's what they were after.

00:34:09.746 --> 00:34:13.117
That's what they were after--
flexibility and hardness.

00:34:13.117 --> 00:34:20.358
And so what is actually
happening in this?

00:34:20.358 --> 00:34:23.327
Well, what's happening
is you're adding

00:34:23.327 --> 00:34:25.496
these covalent bonds between
these very, very long

00:34:25.496 --> 00:34:27.098
strands, right?

00:34:27.098 --> 00:34:34.338
And so you can imagine that if
you pull on that material now,

00:34:34.338 --> 00:34:38.342
well, the long
strands can detangle.

00:34:38.342 --> 00:34:43.147
So in the rubber tree, these
long, long, long, long strands,

00:34:43.147 --> 00:34:44.114
they're all wrapped up.

00:34:44.114 --> 00:34:45.349
It's a spaghetti bowl.

00:34:45.349 --> 00:34:47.819
And I start pulling.

00:34:47.819 --> 00:34:50.088
And they don't break
but they start to kind

00:34:50.088 --> 00:34:54.025
of slide and detangle, right?

00:34:54.025 --> 00:34:56.159
And eventually, you
just break it all apart.

00:34:56.159 --> 00:35:00.531
But now, I've got a lock on
it because each little piece

00:35:00.531 --> 00:35:04.135
of spaghetti is tied to
another one somewhere.

00:35:04.135 --> 00:35:09.373
So I can untangle
them only to a point.

00:35:09.373 --> 00:35:13.311
And then these linkages kick in.

00:35:13.311 --> 00:35:14.612
And they're like, no I'm sorry.

00:35:14.612 --> 00:35:17.715
You can't stretch
anymore, right?

00:35:17.715 --> 00:35:21.519
That's what the crossing does,
it gives it that strength.

00:35:21.519 --> 00:35:25.056
And so you can imagine, if I
had more and more cross-linking,

00:35:25.056 --> 00:35:26.991
I might have a stronger
material, right?

00:35:26.991 --> 00:35:29.694
The stress strain curve
could go like that.

00:35:29.694 --> 00:35:30.761
Not much cross-linking.

00:35:30.761 --> 00:35:33.598
A lot of cross-linking, right?

00:35:33.598 --> 00:35:35.099
Because more and
more of it is going

00:35:35.099 --> 00:35:41.539
to be dictated by these linkages
here between the strands.

00:35:41.539 --> 00:35:43.307
And then there's
something really cool

00:35:43.307 --> 00:35:45.376
that can happen with
cross-linking because this is

00:35:45.376 --> 00:35:47.645
an example of a covalent bond.

00:35:47.645 --> 00:35:49.247
This is covalent.

00:35:53.284 --> 00:35:55.386
But cross-linking can be--

00:35:59.123 --> 00:36:05.897
let's see, it can be
covalent, it can be hydrogen.

00:36:05.897 --> 00:36:09.500
We already saw that there's
sort of natural cross-linking

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:11.335
in nylon because of
that hydrogen bond.

00:36:11.335 --> 00:36:14.539
You can introduce
hydrogen bonds as well.

00:36:14.539 --> 00:36:16.340
You can be purposeful about it.

00:36:16.340 --> 00:36:17.675
By the way, you can have ionic--

00:36:22.613 --> 00:36:27.151
you can put any kind of
bonding that you can dial in,

00:36:27.151 --> 00:36:29.720
and there are different
ways to do that,

00:36:29.720 --> 00:36:32.390
can become a cross-link
opportunity, right?

00:36:32.390 --> 00:36:37.361
So you can just imagine how much
this opened up polymer design.

00:36:37.361 --> 00:36:41.432
How much this cross-linking
opens up polymer design.

00:36:41.432 --> 00:36:47.104
Now, there's a downside as well.

00:36:47.104 --> 00:36:49.540
But first, let's say positive.

00:36:49.540 --> 00:36:51.209
Let's go to your goody bag.

00:36:51.209 --> 00:36:53.911
This is the chemistry inside
your goody bag, right?

00:36:53.911 --> 00:36:58.316
Now, this is a very purposeful.

00:36:58.316 --> 00:36:59.984
Right, these are the
Borax cross-linkers

00:36:59.984 --> 00:37:02.587
you've got that in your
bag and you add it in.

00:37:02.587 --> 00:37:05.489
And what I want you to do
is get an actual feeling

00:37:05.489 --> 00:37:07.858
for the cross-linkers, right?

00:37:07.858 --> 00:37:09.794
And so you can add
more and you'll

00:37:09.794 --> 00:37:11.796
feel the material
change because you're

00:37:11.796 --> 00:37:12.964
linking more and more of it.

00:37:12.964 --> 00:37:15.833
But see, in this case,
it's a hydrogen bond.

00:37:15.833 --> 00:37:17.201
You see that?

00:37:17.201 --> 00:37:18.836
See, those are the
Borax cross-linkers.

00:37:18.836 --> 00:37:23.574
Those groups keep their
OHs and then those OHs,

00:37:23.574 --> 00:37:25.276
there's the polyvinyl alcohol.

00:37:25.276 --> 00:37:28.646
I drew that on the board
somewhere before and those

00:37:28.646 --> 00:37:34.285
OHs can now bond to the
hydrogen or the OH and the PBA

00:37:34.285 --> 00:37:36.320
and that can form a cross-link.

00:37:36.320 --> 00:37:39.156
But there's something
different about this one

00:37:39.156 --> 00:37:40.992
and it's so cool.

00:37:40.992 --> 00:37:45.363
And that's why slime is so
cool and Silly Putty, right?

00:37:45.363 --> 00:37:51.168
Because now there is
a timescale involved.

00:37:51.168 --> 00:37:52.436
There's a timescale involved.

00:37:52.436 --> 00:37:58.209
So now you can
pull it very slowly

00:37:58.209 --> 00:38:00.911
and the hydrogen bonds
that are cross-linking

00:38:00.911 --> 00:38:05.383
haven't have time to
change, to move, to reform.

00:38:05.383 --> 00:38:10.888
Yeah, they break and
then they reform, right?

00:38:10.888 --> 00:38:13.791
But if you pull it really
fast, those hydrogen bonds

00:38:13.791 --> 00:38:15.192
don't have time, right?

00:38:15.192 --> 00:38:18.262
And I'll show you a
picture in a second.

00:38:18.262 --> 00:38:22.366
And so what can happen now
is the mechanical strength

00:38:22.366 --> 00:38:24.035
of this material, the
brittleness of it.

00:38:24.035 --> 00:38:25.936
The properties of it
can literally just

00:38:25.936 --> 00:38:28.139
depend on how
quickly you pull it.

00:38:28.139 --> 00:38:31.709
Because you are up against
the speed of hydrogen bond

00:38:31.709 --> 00:38:33.444
forming and reforming.

00:38:33.444 --> 00:38:36.447
And that is incredibly cool.

00:38:36.447 --> 00:38:39.817
You are literally feeling the
formation, the destruction

00:38:39.817 --> 00:38:42.586
and formation of hydrogen bonds.

00:38:42.586 --> 00:38:43.954
But there's
something else that's

00:38:43.954 --> 00:38:46.624
going on in this
that's also so cool.

00:38:46.624 --> 00:38:53.197
This material, slime, that
polymer is below its glass

00:38:53.197 --> 00:38:55.232
transition temperature.

00:38:55.232 --> 00:38:56.867
Sorry, above.

00:38:56.867 --> 00:38:58.869
I meant above.

00:38:58.869 --> 00:39:00.037
What does that mean?

00:39:00.037 --> 00:39:03.074
It means that without
the cross-linkers,

00:39:03.074 --> 00:39:05.076
that polymer is
a viscous liquid.

00:39:08.646 --> 00:39:10.581
We drew it somewhere.

00:39:10.581 --> 00:39:11.549
I erased it?

00:39:11.549 --> 00:39:14.085
I erased it.

00:39:14.085 --> 00:39:17.154
If you're below the ground the
glass transition temperature,

00:39:17.154 --> 00:39:21.158
you're basically a solid,
like a brittle solid.

00:39:21.158 --> 00:39:25.796
Well, like I drew, polymers
are amorphous, maybe

00:39:25.796 --> 00:39:29.033
with some crystalinity but a lot
of it's going to be amorphous.

00:39:29.033 --> 00:39:31.135
Maybe all of it.

00:39:31.135 --> 00:39:34.705
And so it's going to have a
glass transition temperature

00:39:34.705 --> 00:39:37.708
and if you're above that,
it's this viscous liquid.

00:39:37.708 --> 00:39:41.679
It's this like super
cooled viscous liquid.

00:39:41.679 --> 00:39:43.381
But what I've done
with the cross-linkers

00:39:43.381 --> 00:39:44.949
is I've said hold on.

00:39:44.949 --> 00:39:47.084
You can't go away.

00:39:47.084 --> 00:39:51.956
So I'm literally making
a solid out of liquid.

00:39:51.956 --> 00:39:54.759
We say yeah, just put
like, water in the freezer.

00:39:54.759 --> 00:39:59.296
No, that's not-- the phase
of that polymer is liquid.

00:39:59.296 --> 00:40:01.465
It is a viscous liquid.

00:40:01.465 --> 00:40:05.102
But it is not allowed
to flow or if it does,

00:40:05.102 --> 00:40:06.737
it maybe can change
its shape a little.

00:40:06.737 --> 00:40:09.206
Bit it can't just flow
all over the place because

00:40:09.206 --> 00:40:11.475
of those cross-links, right?

00:40:11.475 --> 00:40:13.277
And that's an elastomer.

00:40:13.277 --> 00:40:19.583
And if you do it just right,
you get a viscal material.

00:40:19.583 --> 00:40:20.684
And what do I mean by that?

00:40:20.684 --> 00:40:22.920
Well, all these things
have to line up, right?

00:40:22.920 --> 00:40:25.956
The thing has to want
to be a liquid so it's

00:40:25.956 --> 00:40:28.225
got to have the right glass
transition temperature.

00:40:28.225 --> 00:40:30.494
But the cross-linkers are
sort of holding it in place.

00:40:30.494 --> 00:40:32.997
And the cross-linker chemistry
has to be the right strength.

00:40:32.997 --> 00:40:36.133
It can't be too strong,
can't be too weak, right?

00:40:36.133 --> 00:40:40.337
And again, by accident, you
might make cross-link silicone.

00:40:40.337 --> 00:40:42.573
Oh there's the goody bag,
which is Silly Putty.

00:40:42.573 --> 00:40:45.142
And that's why I wanted to give
that to you because this hits

00:40:45.142 --> 00:40:46.243
all the right combinations.

00:40:46.243 --> 00:40:51.315
In this case, the backbone is
made out of silicon, right?

00:40:51.315 --> 00:40:52.583
Not carbon.

00:40:52.583 --> 00:40:53.584
But it's the same thing.

00:40:53.584 --> 00:40:56.153
It's the cross-linking
that does this.

00:40:56.153 --> 00:40:58.522
So there's an example of
Silly Putty, which you all

00:40:58.522 --> 00:40:59.390
have the goody bag.

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:05.396
And if you stretch it slowly,
those hydrogen bonds--

00:41:05.396 --> 00:41:09.200
the strands can
kind of pull away.

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:13.337
And those hydrogen bonds have
time to slowly form and reform.

00:41:13.337 --> 00:41:15.706
That's why it kind
of feels the same.

00:41:15.706 --> 00:41:19.410
Nice, elastic right?

00:41:19.410 --> 00:41:21.412
Depending on the
material, on the backbone,

00:41:21.412 --> 00:41:23.113
it might go back as well.

00:41:23.113 --> 00:41:24.648
In Silly Putty, it
kind of just keeps

00:41:24.648 --> 00:41:26.884
on-- those strands
just keep on sliding

00:41:26.884 --> 00:41:28.552
and the hydrogen
bonds keep reforming.

00:41:28.552 --> 00:41:31.589
But if instead I pull it
fast, the hydrogen bonds

00:41:31.589 --> 00:41:32.990
don't have time to react.

00:41:32.990 --> 00:41:36.227
That's why, if you
pull it really fast--

00:41:36.227 --> 00:41:38.195
like OK, don't do this.

00:41:38.195 --> 00:41:40.498
Somebody shot a
bullet through it.

00:41:40.498 --> 00:41:41.532
It breaks like glass.

00:41:44.869 --> 00:41:47.371
And you can feel it in your
hands, just pull really fast.

00:41:47.371 --> 00:41:51.008
The hydrogen bonds don't
have time to reform, right?

00:41:51.008 --> 00:41:52.810
And so what you're left
with is you're just

00:41:52.810 --> 00:41:55.513
breaking apart this material.

00:41:55.513 --> 00:41:59.917
Now, how elastic it feels,
right, how viscous it feels.

00:41:59.917 --> 00:42:01.285
All these things,
these are going

00:42:01.285 --> 00:42:03.888
to be determined by
the glass transition

00:42:03.888 --> 00:42:07.358
temperature of the temperature
of the room, right?

00:42:07.358 --> 00:42:10.327
And so that's going to depend
on whether you actually

00:42:10.327 --> 00:42:12.096
have a very vicious liquid.

00:42:12.096 --> 00:42:13.464
Or maybe you've
got a solid, then

00:42:13.464 --> 00:42:14.698
it's going to be more brittle.

00:42:14.698 --> 00:42:17.568
All of these things are
now at your disposal

00:42:17.568 --> 00:42:19.770
to tune the properties
of the polymer, right?

00:42:19.770 --> 00:42:20.971
They're all at your disposal.

00:42:20.971 --> 00:42:25.409
And it's incredible to think
about, for me at least,

00:42:25.409 --> 00:42:28.979
holding together a liquid
in place like that.

00:42:28.979 --> 00:42:33.284
And that's leads
to viscoelasticity.

00:42:33.284 --> 00:42:35.853
Viscoelasticity, right?

00:42:35.853 --> 00:42:39.523
That's just viscous
liquid in elastic, right?

00:42:39.523 --> 00:42:40.724
OK.

00:42:40.724 --> 00:42:45.162
Now, there's a
couple more things

00:42:45.162 --> 00:42:46.397
that we have to talk about.

00:42:46.397 --> 00:42:47.531
And on Friday.

00:42:47.531 --> 00:42:49.833
What I want to do Friday
is I want to come back

00:42:49.833 --> 00:42:51.869
a little bit to some
of the ways that we're

00:42:51.869 --> 00:42:54.872
trying to maybe
engineer polymers

00:42:54.872 --> 00:43:00.744
to be more like nature, right?

00:43:00.744 --> 00:43:03.247
We were talking
about Monday, if you

00:43:03.247 --> 00:43:05.049
want to try to
solve the problems,

00:43:05.049 --> 00:43:06.483
try to do something big.

00:43:06.483 --> 00:43:08.552
And I gave an example of
an ocean cleanup project.

00:43:08.552 --> 00:43:10.254
But the ocean cleanup
project wouldn't it

00:43:10.254 --> 00:43:12.957
matter if we keep dumping more
and more plastic in the oceans

00:43:12.957 --> 00:43:14.525
that doesn't degrade.

00:43:14.525 --> 00:43:17.428
All right, so we have to think
very carefully about what

00:43:17.428 --> 00:43:18.729
kinds of plastics we can make.

00:43:18.729 --> 00:43:22.333
And the problem with
cross-linking-- this

00:43:22.333 --> 00:43:25.369
gets to the negative
side, right?

00:43:25.369 --> 00:43:27.171
And this is a way
of categorizing

00:43:27.171 --> 00:43:29.173
these things that I've
been talking about

00:43:29.173 --> 00:43:33.477
is if I cross-link it a lot,
we call that a thermoset.

00:43:33.477 --> 00:43:35.079
We call that a thermostat.

00:43:35.079 --> 00:43:35.879
Why?

00:43:35.879 --> 00:43:39.249
Because when you heat it
up, "thermo," it's set.

00:43:39.249 --> 00:43:43.821
So you mold it and then you've
got your little cross-linker.

00:43:43.821 --> 00:43:45.889
You know, you pour
your sulfur in

00:43:45.889 --> 00:43:48.926
and then it's got a very
high number of cross-links.

00:43:48.926 --> 00:43:51.095
And you can see it there, right?

00:43:51.095 --> 00:43:54.031
And it gives you
very strong material.

00:43:54.031 --> 00:43:56.900
But you can't break
it back apart.

00:43:56.900 --> 00:43:59.336
So it's very hard to recycle.

00:43:59.336 --> 00:44:01.005
It's almost impossible.

00:44:01.005 --> 00:44:04.975
If you try to heat these back
up, they just burn usually.

00:44:04.975 --> 00:44:06.677
And if you melt them,
they're not usually

00:44:06.677 --> 00:44:09.013
that useful because they've
got all this other chemistry

00:44:09.013 --> 00:44:11.081
and it completely
messes with the polymer.

00:44:11.081 --> 00:44:12.950
On the other hand,
a thermoplastic

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:15.452
has this kind of
plastic deformation.

00:44:15.452 --> 00:44:18.188
Think maybe like a garbage bag.

00:44:18.188 --> 00:44:20.724
So it can really stretch
and stretch and stretch.

00:44:20.724 --> 00:44:24.028
But it doesn't have
the cross-linkers.

00:44:24.028 --> 00:44:25.362
That's why you can do that.

00:44:28.032 --> 00:44:31.301
And so this is actually much
easier, heat it up, melt it,

00:44:31.301 --> 00:44:32.569
and you can reuse it.

00:44:32.569 --> 00:44:36.040
So that's a recyclable polymer.

00:44:36.040 --> 00:44:39.643
But the issue there
is it's very difficult

00:44:39.643 --> 00:44:43.447
to get all of the necessary
mechanical properties

00:44:43.447 --> 00:44:45.649
from these thermoplastics.

00:44:45.649 --> 00:44:48.185
And so elastomers,
which are maybe

00:44:48.185 --> 00:44:53.323
lightly cross-linked
polymers that

00:44:53.323 --> 00:44:56.560
are above the glass
transition temperature often,

00:44:56.560 --> 00:44:58.629
those are somewhere in between.

00:44:58.629 --> 00:45:02.199
But most of the time, even
if you cross-link lightly,

00:45:02.199 --> 00:45:05.135
it's very difficult to recycle.

00:45:05.135 --> 00:45:08.238
And that's a really good
problem because maybe we

00:45:08.238 --> 00:45:11.842
can come up with
cross-link chemistries that

00:45:11.842 --> 00:45:13.844
are reversible, right?

00:45:13.844 --> 00:45:17.147
They can give us an
opportunity to have

00:45:17.147 --> 00:45:19.416
the best of both worlds.

00:45:19.416 --> 00:45:20.851
It's elastic when we want.

00:45:20.851 --> 00:45:25.289
It's deforms when we want
and it's solid when we want.

00:45:25.289 --> 00:45:28.058
Oh and by the way, you can
degrade all those cross-links

00:45:28.058 --> 00:45:29.493
and reuse it.

00:45:29.493 --> 00:45:34.865
That's like one of the dreams in
making environmentally friendly

00:45:34.865 --> 00:45:36.100
plastic.

00:45:36.100 --> 00:45:38.282
See you guys on Friday.

